INT: Which means?

EK: My mother is not going to school. I am going to school. I should bring the mother. I said, I'm going to school. So he said, [NON-ENGLISH]. Do you know what this means? You fresh. You go home. And if you don't come with your mother, don't come back to school.

So I didn't come back. I didn't want to go back. And then they hired a rabbi with a-- with a very a yellow, not red, beard. And he was coming in and teaching us-- me, anyhow. And I learned that I-- I can daven, I can pray, and I can read, and I can write in Hebrew-- in Jewish. Jewish. Yiddish.

INT: Were your parents active in the Jewish community?

EK: Pardon?

INT: Were your parents active in the Jewish community?

EK: Active? No. They didn't have time for that. My mother had a family to take care of, and my father was busy. And then he was tired, and then-- this was life.

And when I was, like, 14, 15 years old-- 15 years old, I went to work, to a store. I wanted to work. I wanted to bring home some money.

I have worked for a few years there, and I was very happy. People liked me. Matter of fact, I'm still in touch with the daughter of the owner who had the place. Very fine people, nice people.

INT: What were you doing?

EK: Selling materials and coats, whatever there was. They were teaching me how-- way it worked, and slowly. And they liked me there very much. And what then? And then-- then was 1939, I think.

INT: Well--

EK: And the Germans came marching in.

INT: Before-- before that though--

EK: Yeah.

INT: Was your parent-- were you family religious? Did they observe?

EK: My father was going to shul every Shabbos, every Saturday. My mother, not always. But they not fanatics, with kosher. The children all went to cheder.

INT: And what was Shabbat like at home?

EK: Shabbat was a holy evening, Friday night. I know one thing, now with the grandchildren, I tell them, I would never dare get up from the table where my mother and father were sitting.

We had to be excused. You cannot get up and go away. That was what we had to do, and that is what you have to do. And they listen. They do the same. And it was nice.

INT: And you said that your father made goods for three different stores?

EK: Right.

INT: Were there that many stores in the town?

EK: In the town, just one. There was more than one, but he made out of town because they didn't need that much. No, they did. But they bought from him that much, OK? He knew what they needed. He went to [NON-ENGLISH] and Katowice, different stores.

INT: And as far as going to school, to public schools, did you ever experience any antisemitism?

EK: Not-- you know, no. No. I was in a Jewish school. It was for Jewish children, not for goyisha students, just for us, for Jewish.

INT: And aside from school, what did you do? You said you worked when you were 14 or 15. But as a younger child, what were you doing to keep yourself busy?

EK: Helping my mother and helping my older sister. But my older sister didn't have to do so much to begin with. Then she got married. She didn't-- done anything. But she didn't live with us anymore. But I helped my mother with cooking, with baking.

INT: Where did your sister live?

EK: My sister lived in the same town.

INT: So you were able to go to her house and visit?

EK: Yeah. She was coming, we were coming, and to my brother too. But then the war broke out. This was very close to the war. And then my oldest brother-- well, there were a lot of stories.

They were taking the men away from home when the Germans came in, right? And my older brother lived in a-- down-- in a different town. This-- so Sosnowiec was new Sosnowiec and-- and in old Sosnowiec. And we lived in the new Sosnowiec. And my brother, who got married, lived in the old Sosnowiec.

INT: And what was the difference?

EK: The difference-- I don't know. He met a girl from there. There was no difference. The story was that my older brother was caught right-- and going to the Germans already. Is this all right?

INT: Well--

EK: My older brother was caught in the street. He went out for something. He was caught, and we didn't know where he went. My younger brother, with a-- the married brother-- wanted to come because they took-- took father away. And then they brought father back.

They took men and they-- they took him into-- to-- to places. Then they send them home, and we didn't know what's going to be with them. But then my younger brother wanted-- he heard what's going on, but he didn't know that father's back already home.

So he wanted to say goodbye to father, to mother, to talk to them before-- before something happened. And they-- they caught him in the street too. My older brother was someplace already, and he didn't know about it.

And they met together on a truck, on a wagon, someplace. They brought them to the-- on the Russian border. Both brothers, they met there. This one had a child.

My brother had a-- had a child. My sister did. I don't know how to go about further. And which-- I'm going to-- I'm going to start with the Russians, going to go too far. No. I have to go back to-- to myself, right?

I worked. And whoever-- we were called to the Jewish [NON-ENGLISH]. Do you know what this was?

INT: The Jewish--

EK: The Jewish [NON-ENGLISH]. To the-- a Jewish office that they were taking care of-- of the Jews during the war, that the Germans gave them the orders, and the Jews were filling it out.

INT: Now, this--

EK: Like, they need people for camp, concentration camp, right? See, this was the office.

INT: Oh, wait. But what year are we talking about then now?

EK: 1940.

INT: Well, let's-- let's go back before we-- when you said that you were right near the German border. How-- how-- you said you lived right near the German border.

EK: On the German border. Yeah.

INT: Mhm. And even before the war broke out--

EK: Yes.

INT: Did you feel changes in the--

EK: No.

INT: --in the town? Did you feel anything?

EK: No. No.

INT: And the traveling that your father did to other towns, he never brought back any news of anything that was going on?

EK: Not then. No, no. It happened suddenly, everything happened at once then. When the Germans marched into town, then when everything happened, there was no more shopping, no more buying, no more sewing, no more nothing. Everything stopped.

INT: Tell me about the day when the-- when the Germans marched into the town.

EK: When the Germans marched into the town, me and my mother was on a balcony, on the fourth floor-- beautiful flowered balcony with-- with flowers. And-- and we looked down, and we talked loud.

And we looked down and suddenly, we're seeing such giant guys with rifles. And they heard us talk and laugh. We didn't know what-- what this was. We didn't know what was-- they started shooting up there to us.

So we had oleanders, flowers, and they-- they hit a-- a tree. And we got back into the house. My mother and myself, we got back into the house. We closed the door. We didn't know what happened. But then we-- slowly, we found out what happened.

INT: How did you find out what happened after that? After you closed the doors--

EK: After I closed the door-- it was a house. We had neighbors and everybody. And they said, the Germans are coming. The Germans are coming. And we got scared, and we were afraid.

The Germans with-- people from Germany, Jewish people, were sent into Poland before that already. They evacuated them to us. So we knew already something is cooking, but we didn't know what was cooking.

INT: When was that? Do you remember what year those-- the Germans--

EK: 1939.

INT: So those German Jews brought news to you?

EK: Those German Jews came the same year-- or maybe a year before yet, a half a year before yet. And they knew that Hitler's ruling now, and that the Jews are not well off.

INT: And when they were evacuated into your town, some of them, where did they live? How did they--

EK: They were in different-- I need a tissue. Oh, they didn't give me a-- yeah. May I?

INT: Yes.

EK: Not in private homes. They didn't live in private homes. They put them into, like, camps. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Nobody could go to family, if they had any, but seldom did they have family.

INT: And were you able to speak with any of them, any of the--

EK: No. I didn't. I didn't have a chance.

INT: Or your mother?

EK: I don't think so. We were watching out for ourselves. We were afraid.

INT: So at the point when those German Jews came into your town, that's when you started to realize--

EK: What's going to happen. Yeah. Because they were telling us, but I didn't thought--

INT: Mhm. And in school, did they talk about it at all?

EK: There was no school anymore. Finished.

INT: OK. And you said that-- that on that day then, the Germans came in. And you were-- you were watching from the balcony.

EK: Right.

INT: And what happened later on that evening?

EK: They were coming in more, and more, and more. So we knew they were in town already. That same day, nothing happened. Across from our-- where we lived, the home, was a school, a big school.

Not a Jewish-- a Gentile school. And there were soldiers up and down. All over, we seen soldiers with uniforms and everything. And the next day, we were told that we have to move. The army is going into the building. We have to move.

INT: They came to your house and told you you had to move?

EK: They didn't come. Somebody came-- not the soldiers-- somebody came and told us that we have to move. And before we had time to move or to do anything, they started throwing out things from the windows, from the balcony, down-- mattresses, furniture, throwing--

INT: From your house?

EK: --into the street. Right. And then we had to leave. And then I-- I don't remember how it was-- it was-- but we had to look for an apartment someplace.

INT: And where did you find an apartment?

EK: Oh, we found one. It didn't take-- it took long, but the-- we got an apartment in a different state.

INT: Now, was your father still able to work at this point?

EK: No. No. That stopped.

INT: And how were you able to afford the apartment?

EK: I don't know what to tell you. Probably had some money saved up.

INT: And it was much smaller, the apartment?

EK: The first one? It wasn't bad. Then we had to leave this one too, and the second one wasn't bad either. No, the worst was the ghetto. But it isn't-- wasn't done yet. But we went through three apartments in the-- in town, different apartments.

INT: And what were the days like during the times that you were moving from one apartment to the other? You couldn't go to school. Your father didn't work. What were the days like?

EK: Well, you really don't know how to describe it to you. It wasn't good. It was bad. We were always scared. We were always afraid of everything.

INT: Were you able to go outside?

EK: Oh, then, yes. Yeah. Yes. We were able, but you-- we didn't want to go.

INT: What was going on in the street?

EK: We were afraid of Germans. Nothing was going on.

INT: Were people disappearing?

EK: Not that I knew about.

INT: Did you have to wear--

EK: Not yet. Yeah, we did, later on, a Jewish star, one in the front and one in the back.

INT: So you moved to three different apartments.

EK: Right.

INT: And basically kept to yourselves.

EK: Right.

INT: You had a shop, though, for food. Did you help your mother in shopping for food?

EK: My mother and my father took care of this.

INT: Did you play with friends?

EK: I still worked there. If I played with friends? No. We didn't go to see friends

INT: So every day was spent inside?

EK: I really don't know how to describe it. I don't remember it, exactly how it was. The whole thing, I know, but--

INT: And so when did things start changing drastically?

EK: About then. [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS] Well, I still worked in the store. I forgot about that now. The store had-- the Jewish people had the store. The Jewish people were chased out, and they came in a German guy, who was a friend of the people who owned the store. So they were-- [? Grieser ?] was their name.

INT: Drieser?

EK: [? Grieser. ?]

INT: [? Grieser. ?]

EK: And they got a German who they liked and trusted. The Germans take over the business and run it for them. And people could work there. And this was called-- the guy, the German guy, was called a treuhander. Treuhander. I don't know what that means really.

INT: OK.

EK: He was managing the store. As long as the German was there, they could have the store, but the German was running it. Because people were still coming shopping, and I worked there for a while. I had-- I'm not telling the story right.

INT: I'd say you're telling it--

EK: Back and forth.

INT: That's fine.

EK: Right. And then they started calling us for-- for-- to go to-- to camp. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] They were grabbing people-- people in the street, young people.

My friend and her sister went for a bread, and she never came back. She was taken in a truck, taken on a truck. I've never seen them again. And there was one more sister, a third sister, and I was friendly with those girls, with the three. And a brother of hers was taken.

And other people were being grabbed in the street. The ones that were being grabbed in the street, we didn't know that they're going for death right away. They were all-- they were taken right away to Auschwitz.

And I had notices to come in register. Everybody had notices. I had notices to come and register. But what I was working, I was told-- I'm not going to be sent anyplace because I work, and I work for the German treuhander.

So I didn't know how many months it was that I-- they called me twice, and they let me go home. I don't remember if were in the ghetto yet then. I don't-- I really don't remember. But they let me go back home, so my parents were at ease that I came back.

And-- twice. It just didn't go on a week. It-- months. At that time, when I went to register, they called. They sent me a note, and I had to come to register. And they-- I couldn't go home.

This was 1943, I think. '39-- it was '43 already. In between, there was life going on, the way it was going on. In 1943, I didn't come back. Instead of going home, they sent me in a [? Skladowa, ?] [? Skladowa. ?]

This was a [NON-ENGLISH], a-- I don't know what you call this. A place where they took the people in to send them away to camps.

INT: Like a transit camp?

EK: Right. A transit camp. Right. It was very crowded, a lot of people.

INT: Before we get to [? Skladowa ?] though, I just wanted to ask. You said that they were picking people up off of the street and taking them to Auschwitz.

EK: The ones--

INT: Did you know about Auschwitz at that point?

EK: We heard about it. We heard about it. We knew. We heard about it but, we didn't-- nobody believed it. But we had no-- I think I knew later about it. I'm talking now about it, but I didn't know then probably.

But we knew all the people who were picked up by-- by this SS or whatever, they went to Auschwitz to the ovens. This we found out really later. But I knew they never came back.

But I also knew that if I go to camp to work, I'm not going to go to the gas chambers. And if I don't go, if they catch me-- but in the meantime, I was working. But then they said, no. No more. They cannot delay me anymore. I have to go to arbeits camp, to a camp to work.

INT: Arbeits camp.

EK: Arbeits camp. Right.

INT: Let me ask you just one other question. You said that they were picking people off of the street, and I asked you about the population of-- the Jewish population. How many people? How many Jewish people were in Sosnowiec?

EK: A lot.

INT: Can-- a million? I mean--

EK: No, no, no, no, no, no. Not that many. No.

INT: OK.

EK: It wasn't such a big town.

INT: Right.

EK: Maybe 30,000.

INT: 30,000 Jews?

EK: I think so. Between 20,000 and 30,000, I don't know how many.

INT: OK.

EK: There were Jews. And when I was sent to [? Skladowa-- ?] I think I mentioned it already-- was that terrible.

INT: Yes. And how far was that from Sosnowiec?

EK: To [? Skladowa? ?] It was in Sosnowiec, downtown. Downtown. Downtown, OK? From downtown. And it was where they were getting people together, when the Germans first started picking up people, and putting them together-- the Jews.

And putting together, then they let them go. They they-- but then this was for camp only. And it was terrible, a lice-infected place. I was there for two nights, and there were lice, and lice. And when I went out of there, I didn't know what to do with myself. And then they sent me from there to Gogolin.

INT: So at this point, you've left your family.

EK: Yeah. They never seen me again.

INT: And you went to Gogolin?

EK: First to [? Skladowa. ?]

INT: Mhm.

EK: Then to Gogolin, to a durgangslager, to a-- also a holding like camp.

INT: And repeat what it was called.

EK: Gogolin.

INT: No, but you said 'slaga?' What-- what did you just say? What was the name you just--

EK: Lager? It wasn't a concentration camp lager. It's a durgangslager. This is a holding camp, just you told me. It's a holding camp, another holding camp.

Over there, it wasn't this dirty, there was no lice. And we got cleaned up. And what did we do there? Different things. We stayed there for a while, for a few weeks.

INT: What kind of different things? Like what?

EK: Like cleaning the toilets, which were just boards outside in this-- in the-- in the field. Whatever they could give us, everything for Jews. Everything that was bad to do there we did it. We had to do it, you know? We-- OK.

But from there-- we stayed there maybe two weeks, or three weeks. I don't know. A few weeks. I really don't-- I'm very bad with numbers, with dates. And then somebody came to select us for Gleiwitz [NON-ENGLISH], to send us to Gleiwitz, to an arbeits camp.

Those were all not arbeits camps, not working camps-- just to hold us-- to hold-- hold over. And then they sent us-- send us to Gleiwitz [NON-ENGLISH]. In Gleiwitz [NON-ENGLISH], they selected us again. And-- [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

They assigned us to different things. They assigned me to field work, to wheat. We take wheat out and do different things. And not me-- everybody does something else. What was then-- then later--INT: Today is Sunday, May 5, 1996. And we're interviewing Eda Klepfisz. Eda, just to go back in time just a bit, when the Germans came in, in 1939, you were 18. And from that time until 1943, when you did go to the transit camps, there was a point where you lived in the ghetto with your family.

EK: Right.

INT: You mentioned.

EK: Right.

INT: Can you-- do you remember some of that? Can you describe some--

EK: Yes. Yes, we lived in a very small two rooms. Also dirty, filthy. Somebody moved out, poor people, poor, poor and dirty people.

And they gave this to my mother, father, three brothers, not the little ones. Victor, Meir, and maybe-- I don't know-- three of them were going out every day, 6 o'clock in the morning, to work in the streets, to clean the streets. From the camp-- from the-- from the ghetto.

Also, we had no beds to sleep on. We had straw sacks, which was straw filled up with, with something. And my sister and her husband and their child. And my mother and my father and my younger brothers, we all slept there in that room. But what is a nice thing, I just met somebody in this country who is from our town, who I knew the family.

And he told me that he was with the militia. Not policia, militia, to, to go and check out the houses at the ghettos, how the people take care of them. My mother got rewards three times. She was the cleanest woman in the-- in the street.

And this is somebody who I know from home, the parents. And I met him in Florida. And he-- and I said, you lying, you cheating me.

I said, I knew I had a clean mother and a good mother. He said no, he was with the Germans. He was going around.

They made him some, some-- something there. And three times, my mother was the cleanest. She had rewards for being the cleanest.

My little brothers were going out, 6:00 in the morning, my younger-- three brothers, I had. And they were coming home late at night, hungry, frozen, whatever it was. This was the ghetto.

Bread, if you had a few dollars-- zlotys, you could buy a bread. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Was I working yet then? I don't know.

I don't working, or was I working? If I was working I brought home some money too. And if I didn't work, I didn't.

INT: Was the ghetto closed at night?

EK: The ghetto, no. It was-- it wasn't open, but it wasn't closed. But you weren't free to, to roam around or to go here or there, anywhere.

INT: Describe the ghetto. How big was it? How many blocks? How--

EK: Not big. Not big. Blocks, it was a few blocks, which was houses, that they took, put us, took us in there. I don't know exactly what size.

INT: Were people taken away constantly from the ghetto?

EK: No. Not when I was there, no. No.

INT: Do you remember what year you were there? How old you were?

EK: It was 17, 18, 19, I don't know. Maybe not. Maybe I wasn't that old yet. 17, 18 maybe. I don't know. I really don't know.

INT: Well, 18 would make it 1939. So--

EK: 1939 was when the Germans came in.

INT: Right.

EK: This is later.

INT: Right.

EK: So I had to be older. Whatever.

INT: OK. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] were you-- was there a school going on in the ghetto? Were there--

EK: No, not for Jews. Nothing for Jews.

INT: So your brothers didn't go to school? They just, they just worked from early in the morning till early at night?

EK: They had to go out and clean the streets. Right.

INT: And did they get paid for that?

EK: No, no. Not to eat either.

INT: And not to eat.

EK: No.

INT: They were just forced to go out and clean the streets?

EK: To work, right.

INT: Do you remember how long you were in the ghetto?

EK: Well, in 1943, I was taken to-- going to camp.

INT: OK, so when you were taken to the camp, your, your family was still in the ghetto?

EK: Yes. Yes. And they were taken away from the ghetto later on. I had, in camp already, somebody, some Polack came. Somebody, somebody had news that they took all the Jews away. They took them to Auschwitz. That they took them--

INT: So did you think that your whole family perished?

EK: We didn't know anything. They said, they took all the Jews away. And the story was a Polack brought it.

