So there was a Zeidengarten, women. Her name was Zeidengarten. There were two sisters.

One was a lady who was married, married. I don't if the husband left her or whatever, and a sister. Was-- this was, was Sasa, too. And the sister was [? Wanda, ?] the sister, younger sister.

And then came another girl, Silberberg. I forgot the first name, but the last thing was Silberberg. Maybe [? Anja. ?] I don't know, Silberberg. I knew her. I knew all the girls. She said, I want to go with you too. OK?

I said, fine. But I want to get off the train. I'm not going any further. So that older sister said to the younger sister, you know, I wanted to go. But I want you to go. You're younger.

I used to vacation here with my husband. She named the name, we knew [NON-ENGLISH]. I knew him. I knew the husband. I knew her.

From very fine, fine and very rich and very nice people. So she said, I am-- maybe I am too old. Wanda, you jump with Eda. Go with Eda.

She says, I used to vacation in Czechoslovakia. And they're most wonderful people. They're going to help you. And suddenly she pulled out from someplace, I don't know, a strand of beautiful pearls. They were opening up your eyes.

Where did she have this from they? They, they, they checked for everything. They told us if we don't-- yeah, in camp, in camp they said, we had to bring out all the pictures of the family, and to burn them on a-- on a stake, burn, everybody.

So they told us, Jews have diamonds. You have-- Jews have gold. Jews have pearls. Jews have everything.

But you don't have it, you're in camp. You don't have it. But you have pictures of your families.

We want all the pictures out. And if we find a picture in anybody's house, in anybody's pocket, in anybody-- you'll be shot. So in one day-- this was before that. Because I remember it now.

One day everybody brought out the pictures. And they was putted in a stack of fire. And they burned everything. That's why I don't have any pictures. Nobody from my camp.

And here she has pearls. I was thinking, where does she have those pearls from? She hid it. Probably hide them, hid them someplace that she-- she says for this you're going to find people. They pay you for it. You're going to have bread for all the girls.

So there was only three to go. And that train was standing. Then the train started going. And one of the women said, but you got to go first, Eda.

I said, fine. I go first. I didn't care if I'm going to live, if I'm going to die. I was all, given up life. Then I couldn't go up there because they're going to shoot me. But somebody had to go and lay down with the back.

And when the train is going to start I'm going to throw myself over and down was snow. Snow, snow, snow, snow, snow. And it was like a-- not a mountain. Like, what do you call this?

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I don't know how to explain it, where the train runs. And there was a lot of snow on the side. What was, if there were stones or whatever, I didn't know. I know I'm going to throw myself into the snow.

I couldn't jump. I had to throw myself with the coat, with warm shoes, with warm boots. The bread we don't-- bread was gone. 'You go first.' I said, OK, I go first.

And this Wanda, she kissed her sister. She hugged her and kissed her. And she said to the sister, to her, don't worry. Eda wanted to go. She knows what she's doing.

I don't know how, she just knew. And 'You'll be fine. Czechs are wonderful people. You'll be fine. You sell the pearls to them or give them to them. They give you food. You have for everybody.' OK.

That Sasa, like, sat down. And they helped me go on her back. And I threw myself over. The train was running. And there was shots. So many shots.

And I've seen a big-- a fire thing, that like fire, right? I said, oh, I'm dying, to myself, and I died. I was at ease, I died. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And I heard more shots and more shots and more shots. The other girls jumped.

To make the story short, they got killed. The ones that run first, they noticed me. And they were too late to kill me. The others, they killed.

Because they were in the wagons behind us, right behind us. I didn't know until after the war. Nobody knew in the train, alive or sick or whatever.

Oh, I don't know how long I was laying there. A day or two, or a night or hours, I don't know. But suddenly, I heard a train. I was alive. I don't know what I was-- who I was, where I was. I heard a train.

When I heard a train, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I started thinking. Who am I? Who am I? I didn't know who I am or what I was and what I did. I didn't know nothing.

But I heard a train. The train, the train went by. And I started to, to move, to move, to move. I touched I think my face.

It felt sticky like. I touched my face. It was-- I didn't know what it was, but it all cracked up and bloody. This I have seen later. I didn't know then.

But it was cracked up and bloody. But I knew I am alive. But who am I? What happened? How am I alive?

And I was frozen like a block of ice. Somehow, from the snow, I pushed myself up and I pushed myself to a side. And I went this way and I went this way. Not go, but push myself.

I was like a piece of ice, like a block of ice. And my hands were hurting. That's why, why I came home with those things.

And I started rolling and tried to get up. Finally I got on my feet. Got on my feet. But they were hardly-- they were waggly. They wouldn't want to stand.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And I think another train went by. I think so, on top of me there. But I was-- I don't know-- I don't know who I am. I don't know who I am, what I am.

But I started standing up. And I started walking up the hill. The snow, snow, snow, snow, all over. It was like a-- I don't know how to explain it in English, I don't know.

Where the-- the trains are running on top, and this is-- and I started walking and walking, who am I? What am I? Who am I?

What am I? Where am I? Who am I? I didn't know who I am.

And I walk more and more and I got stronger. I felt stronger. And I was up that hill. And I've seen tracks, railroad tracks. And I walk and I walk on the side of the railroad, railroad track.

But I was thinking if a train comes, it's going to kill me. So I tried to walk away. And if I will hear-- this, I was conscious already. I don't know how, that if will hear, I'll let myself down again.

But no train came then when I walked there. I walked and walked and walked. And suddenly, I see a, a, a cabin.

Like the, the train, the people that sit there and change the, the, the lines and everything. And I wanted to get there already. And I did. Somehow I did. I got there to that.

And there's a older man, not old, an older man, sitting there. And he got up and stuck his head out. And I said [SPEAKING GERMAN]. German. I spoke German very well. [SPEAKING GERMAN].

He looks at me. He looks at me. He says, come in. In German, [GERMAN].

My legs didn't work, but somehow I got into that booth. He picked up a big container, a big one, like for a horse. And held it and started feeding me water. And I started drinking. I drank out that whole-- it was about 10 quarts there. And I said, [GERMAN], more water, more wasser, more wasser? 'Nein,' no more wasser.'

He-- I put my hands-- I had a coat on, and pockets. And I had two cakes of soap in my pockets that we used to get every week for washing up. I took out those-- I'm too late? I took out two cakes of soap and I gave them to him. And I said, more wasser, more water.

He said, [GERMAN]. And he gives me back those. I said, [SPEAKING GERMAN]. So he took. And then he picks up a telephone and calls somebody.

And then take maybe 20 minutes. There came a man with a elegant velour coat, with a velour hat. With a swastika.

