EK: Yeah. About, I don't know how many were there. A long, long line. And they were all with their back to me. When somebody walks in, like to the back.

But they all turned around their chairs. And they started asking me who I am and what I am. So two came over. They're going to take care of me. OK.

So they start and asked me who I am. I said, I'm a Volksdeutsche, and I was evacuated from my hometown. All the Volksdeutsches were evacuated. How will they find out? That's what I was thinking.

To begin with, I didn't know who I was. And then I got back a little bit to myself. And-- and I was with my mother.

A door opened up and I fell out. And here I am, laying on the-- on the tracks, on the railroad tracks. And I think I-- I was-- I, I was-- I didn't-- wasn't run over.

But I think I passed out. Because I don't know nothing, what happened. I knew I fell out. I knew and I fell out from the-- from the--

