And she put a bath on for me. I should go get undressed and get washed. He told her to clean me up and to-- as she listened. She told me she's not listening to him. But she has a human being in front of her.

And she told him-- he told her that I have a number and I'm Jewish. And she had this, so she wanted to help me more yet. Even she had the general on the frontier.

She put me in a bathtub. I could hardly get in and get out. And she washed me up.

She's sponged me. She washed my face. She washed my hair. She-- she dried me.

She brought me-- and she-- she put through-- at the table she put a robe on me and slippers. Like as if she would have a baby there. And I was not bigger than a baby then, you know?

But I was dirty, and I-- she brought some chicken soup. Where she had this from, I don't know. She had soup. She had chicken soup. She brought me chicken soup. She want me to eat that soup and then I should lay down.

And I listened to her. I didn't know who knows what. I listened to her. She seemed good. She seemed nice.

When I was laying down, then she said, it's time to get up. Because Ladia-- is that-- that Gestapo, that Ladia Kepak. Ladia-- Ladia's going to come with a doctor to remove a number that you have on your arm. I don't know how they're going to do it, but he's coming here, so you have to be dressed. He probably called him between. Who knows?

Well, I was-- I had-- had dress on. I had a pair of slippers on. I felt like a human being again. And what else did I have? I don't know what else.

And then came the two men. Yeah, she gave me soup. Then she gave me a tea with crackers. And that's it. You know, this was enough. I had enough.

And then the-- the men came here, opened the door, comes in. He was very tall. And the other was a head taller than him.

And he looked like a-- [NON-ENGLISH] nobody. Not a doctor. And they want to see my-- my number.

And she has seen it already. I had 79,327. In between was a cross, because they made a mistake. So they put a cross in there and they added the number 79,327.

And that guy sits down. And he talks to the woman. And the woman brings a bottle of whisky.

And-- and the other one says for him too, two bottles of whisky. And here is a oven-- coal oven. And they have a-- a curling roller. The man, the doctor, a curling roller. And drinks whisky. Sticks that curling roller into the-- to the oven, to the--

INT: A curling roller?

EK: A curling roller for hair. That's what they-- this was the doctor. And started going over my hand.

It burned it. It-- it was enough one touch. The number will be done. They did it three times-- twice. The third time, I've seen the flesh. I said, please, [GERMAN SPEECH]. [GERMAN SPEECH] This was his expression, 'I let you swim.' So he said, I let you swim again.

You know when he-- I-- when I didn't want to undress, he says he lets me swim. Like, you don't-- don't want to do it my way, I let you swim until you drown. That's what I-- that's [INAUDIBLE] I translated it to. But I couldn't take it. I felt my heart is going three times.

Took a bandage. Nothing. Bending and bending it around. The whiskey was finished. They both went. And he said-- she said-- he said something to the women again. To that woman on ear.

He went out. And he said, this is [GERMAN SPEECH]. This is not-- not my friend. He's going to bring you to a different house to sleep over tonight, because you cannot sleep here. Supposedly he's coming over there. I don't know. That's how it looked to me.

In the building. She takes 50 Deutsche Mark and shows me. And she says, put this in a pocket. Put this wherever you want. Hide it. Don't let him know that you have it.

And promise me one thing. Wherever you have the first chance to run away from him, run away. Please run away. If the 50 Marks will help you, let it help you. I haven't got more to give you. But run away from him.

So he came back, [NON-ENGLISH]. And he took me in the same building to a different house with a key. And there was like a waitress dressed up, with three kids in bed, sleeping.

And he tells her a story too on the ear. And he leaves. And he says he's coming back tomorrow for me. She should put me to sleep and he's coming back tomorrow. He told her that. And she said OK. He laying down on the couch.

The next morning, he came. He said-- I was dressed. And dressed [INAUDIBLE] that's what the lady had, in this coat and top and the shoes. And I-- and she said, it's going to-- we're going to walk. It's out of town.

You're going to walk behind me. Don't look left. Don't look right. This was his-- always his saying. Just follow me and don't look around. I know what I'm doing.

I want to help you. I want to get in touch with your father. I want to help you. Good.

Going, going, going. Like you know, the town. And suddenly he stops, and he says, [GERMAN]. Stay-- stay here.

He started picking up rubbles, stones, little stone and throwing them in a window and throwing them in a window. Up, upstairs. Looks like a-- like a split level.

And suddenly, somebody opens a window. And a head sticks out, black, dark, like a gypsy. I got a little scared. What can I do? I'm going to sleep here, he said.

Takes me into the house. She went down, opened the door. Takes me into the house. And I see an old lady, a real gypsy, an old one with long hair. And a guy, a real gypsy.

And this was really like a split level in America. Not a-- not a things that the-- the gypsies go, with the wagons. And he says I have to go upstairs with him. So I went upstairs with him.

And he left. He told her something too again, the-- the-- the gypsy. And he left. And he said he's coming back. To me, 'I'll be back.' [GERMAN SPEECH], good.

When he left again there, they started talking to me. The old gypsy started looking at me. She came up.

And she said, where are you from? Kepak told his-- her daughter that I am from Poland. Kepak is the Gestapo.

I said yes, I'm from Poland. 'What town?' I said, Sosnowiec. I say, warum, warum-- why do you ask me? I didn't know how to talk to the gypsy.

But I-- she says, [POLISH], I am from Poland-- the gypsy. Polska, she says, from Poland. But she doesn't-- doesn't write Polish. 'What happened? How did you get here?' in Polish.

Then she tells me, she lived in Cracow-- Cracow. A tabor of gypsies came to town. She was a little girl. She was seven years old.

And a gypsy picked her up and put her into the tabor, into the wagon. And she, 'And I didn't want to go out. And then they went away with the horses and I went with them. And when I was--'

She spoke Polish, but very broken-- very broken, but I understood every word. When I was about 7, 8 or 9 or 10, she says to me in Polish, he married me. And this is my husband, the old guy.

[SIGHS]

So what are we doing now? I went to sleep, with the gypsy, in bed. Then the mother tells me she had a husband, a gypsy. The Germans took him away and they killed him on the way.

Where they took him, they killed him. And they threw him out someplace. He was found.

So now that Kepak comes to the wife. And she hates him, the mother. She hates him. He's a German. He's not a Czech.

And I slept over the night. I didn't sleep. That day I didn't sleep. I laid all night.

And the next day they told me that they have to begin with work, the gypsies, OK. It didn't happen here yet. Something else happened. They, they-- they told me-- the old mother said, we will do something for you to help you. We will do something for you to help you.

Then they introduce me a son whose name was Eda. Eda. The son's name was Eda. So they're going to call me Heda and him Eda, because they know Eda, because he is Eda.

And they have another son. I forgot the name of the other son. I remembered it yesterday. I don't remember it now. Another son.

And they take all the gypsies away. And-- but why didn't they take their sons yet? Because, as she calls the younger one, and he shows me he has cut up things, and has corn or something, some wheats put in there.

And it's-- it's-- and it's puss in it. Then she calls the older one, Heda-- Eda, to show the same thing. I say, why?

Why is this? That you go through it? Polish, why?

She says, if they will be clean they will take him and kill him. But they have sores, the [NON-ENGLISH]. They have-- they had-- they have germs. So they won't take him.

The germs are going to be gone, they come and take him. But the germs would try to do what we can, the germs should be there. And the germs are there.

They got to eat there too. They gave me something to eat. And I went to sleep with the gypsy.

And in the morning, the old gypsy came up. And she took me down. And she talked to me, and she talked to me. And she talked to me.

And she told me I have to get away from that guy-- the gypsy, the old gypsy. That if the husband would live, he would shoot him. No, she said, he would cut his throat. He would cut him here. But-- but [POLISH], in Polish. He would cut his throat.

They started working on this, to get me out of this house. The gypsies started working on this, to get me out of the house. And they told me they're going. But the next day he's supposed to come. And he came.

INT: He-- the-- the German?

EK: Yes, yeah, the Gestapo. He came. But before he came, they open-- put the-- a credenza. Pulled up a credenza away from the wall.

And there's a big hole. Passing [INAUDIBLE] in there. Those are gypsies. And they told me, Eda, [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] you got to go in there and sit there. Because he's going to come tonight. And he's going to look for you.

And my daughter is in love with him. And he told her to treat you good and to make you healthy. He wants you. [INAUDIBLE] the daughter told mother, mother should help her. So she's helping the daughter-- helping me, not the daughter.

I sat in there. They closed it. He came. He didn't come that day.

They opened it, yes, for me, because he didn't come. He came another day. He came with another guy, with a Gestapo. Not a-- not a doctor. The doctor, wasn't a doctor.

And she said that [GERMAN SPEECH], I run away. She doesn't know. And she cried [INAUDIBLE]. I heard her cry. Cried loud, the old mother. They don't know how, but I ran away.

And they looked all over. They looked everywhere. They cannot find me.

He said, I'm going to find her. He started shooting. He didn't shoot in that wall. He probably didn't know about this.

He was shooting in the up and down and back and forth, and the other Gestapo with him. They were shooting. And finally they left. They left.

For a daytime, I had to sit in there. For a night, I could go out of there. They fed me. They gave me to eat.

They-- they helped me with to-- to-- to-- they send their son to the drugstore to buy something. But then they were afraid they shouldn't think that they're hiding somebody. So different stories I heard. Anyway, they brought whatever they brought. That hand got swollen like this, overnight.

The next day-- he came and shooting, shooting, shooting, shooting, he didn't find me. The next day they said, you got to go to another house. You can't stay here no more. You can't stay here anymore. Why can't I stay here anymore?

'You can't. Because he's going to find you. He's going to kill us all. Or the Gestapo's going to find us.' So they said the next day that I go next door.

They take me on a bicycle. And they bring me to-- to some, to a friend of his. And they-- and I am going to be safe there-- very safe.

And from there, they're going to take me to a daughter who lives in a different town, to another daughter who is married and has three children. I'm going to take there when the [GERMAN SPEECH]. And I'm going to be a maid for the daughter.

The next day, they took me on a bike. And they took me to that house. Running over the-- and afraid, very scared. Because after all, who did they have on that bike? Two brothers went, one after the other.

And go by a door. And knock on the door. And a door opens up. And there is a Gestapo in a uniform-- the whole uniform, from the helmet to the bottom.

And I thought he's going to shoot me. When he looks at me he says, my god, Diese Augen, deise Augen. That's what I remember-- the eyes. Those eyes; I probably looked like a scared rabbit.

He said, those Augen, these-- those eyes, those eyes, those eye-- and I look around and I say, what-- what did you do to me? What did you do to me? You brought me--

He started telling me that this guy is hiding out from the German army. They took him in. So-- because their father works for the Germans and they want to save the father. So they're helping that German. And he's going to save the father. But I'm not going to stay here. But that night I had to stay there.

And what else should I tell you? [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It's not the end yet. Far, not. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I've seen-- I-- I got-- I quiet down a little bit. I was sure this is Gestapo, and he's going to-- going to shoot me. They're going to get rid of me.

But the-- 'Diese Augen, diese Augen, diese Augen.' I didn't know what he wants from mine Augen. Mine-- my, my Augen-- mine eyes were swollen, maybe, or scared or whatever.

