It was so black. But it looked like soil, not like a floor. It was a floor.

Well, I started scrubbing that floor every day until this floor got white-- every day on the day. I slept with the three children in a little bed. I was broken up like I don't know what. But I was grateful too.

The children had lice-- all three of them. I-- they told me. Mommy, can't-- the mother cannot do it.

I've learned a little Czech while I was there. Because I didn't-- didn't happen the same day that I left there. And Polish and Czech is very familiar. Very, this, almost the same. So I've learned a little Czech already.

So what did we do? Start-- we started cleaning the children's heads. Every days I asked her if she couldn't buy a comb to do it. She says [INAUDIBLE] no. No. Leave them alone.

I said, how can they go to school? How are they going to go to school?

One boy was Milan. One girl was Vanka. And the third one I don't remember, also a girl. Milan and Vanka. The third one I don't remember. I-- it's going to come to me if I think.

She said, the school doesn't care. And I shouldn't do it either. But I did. And I told her. And the children told her, [? Mommy, ?] the head doesn't itch anymore. I cleaned them all three up. Once I had-- and the-- in that camp, in that first camp, the-- the lice, and here, again, lice.

And I got to like those children. OK.

